{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"06","chapter_title":"AI Conversations as a Teacher","title":"Reflection on Teacher Judgment","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting reflection on teacher judgment to a decision log, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-01","statement":"Convert a feelings paragraph into a decision log with one row per AI suggestion (situation, what the AI said, decision, reason)"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-02","statement":"Judge whether a reason is defensible by testing if it points to a standard a reader could re-apply, rather than a mood"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-03","statement":"Distinguish ACCEPT, OVERRIDE, and REJECT, and log a rejected unrequested AI addition"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-04","statement":"Read the accept/override ratio as evidence of weighing rather than blanket trust or contrarianism"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher","learner_reflection","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/04/05"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_calibration","cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_reject_row"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_artifact_signal"],"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-02":["cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_reject_row"],"cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-04":["cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Reflection on Teacher Judgment\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Learner Reflection, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-06-reflection-on-teacher-judgment.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Reflection on Teacher Judgment: Learner Reflection, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost reflections students write are feelings paragraphs: \"I learned a lot, the AI was helpful, I feel more confident.\" That kind of reflection proves nothing, because it contains no decisions a reader can examine. This section teaches you to reflect by logging the moments where you accepted or overrode AI advice, with the reason for each, so your judgment becomes countable instead of merely claimed.\n\nJudgment shows up at the override points. Anytime you take AI advice without thinking, no judgment was exercised; anytime you reject or change it, you made a call you can be asked to defend. A decision log captures those calls in rows: the situation, what the AI said, what you decided, and why. The ratio of overrides to accepts becomes a piece of evidence on its own, showing whether you were steering or just following.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Convert a feelings paragraph into a decision log with one row per AI suggestion (situation, what the AI said, decision, reason).\n- Judge whether a reason is defensible by testing if it points to a standard a reader could re-apply, rather than a mood.\n- Distinguish ACCEPT, OVERRIDE, and REJECT, and log a rejected unrequested AI addition.\n- Read the accept/override ratio as evidence of weighing rather than blanket trust or contrarianism.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nTwo students submit reflections on the same week of AI work. The first writes: \"Using AI was really helpful this week and I feel more confident. I will keep using it.\" The second writes a four-row log: the AI told them to write \"tell the parent their child failed,\" and they overrode it because the tone was too harsh for a first quiz; the AI told them to teach fractions before decimals, and they accepted it because it matched the pasted standards; the AI told them to say a student was \"unmotivated,\" and they overrode it because motivation is not observable.\n\nThe first reflection is unfalsifiable; nothing in it could be wrong. The second can be audited line by line, and it shows the student overrode the AI twice on principle and accepted it once on evidence. Only the second demonstrates judgment.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA decision log records judgment as a set of discrete calls, each with a reason that points to a standard.\n\n| Column | What it captures | Why it matters |\n|--------|------------------|----------------|\n| Situation | the moment a decision was needed | anchors the call in something concrete |\n| AI said | the advice given | shows what you were judging against |\n| I decided | ACCEPT or OVERRIDE | makes the call explicit |\n| Why | the reason, tied to a standard | converts a preference into a defensible judgment |\n\nThe reason column is where judgment lives. \"Override, too harsh\" is weak; \"override, because the advice assumed a cause I cannot observe\" points to a principle that would apply again. Good reasons reference an observable standard (the pasted standards, what you can actually see, the audience's needs), not just personal taste.\n\nThe accept/override ratio is itself evidence. A log that is all accepts suggests you followed without judging; a log that is all overrides may suggest you ignored useful help. A mix, each with a reason, is the signature of someone weighing advice rather than rubber-stamping or reflexively rejecting it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A log row reads: AI said ’teach fractions before decimals,’ I decided OVERRIDE, Why: ’it just felt wrong.’ Why does this row fail the reason check?\" explanation=\"The reason ’it felt wrong’ would be equally true if the student had decided ACCEPT instead, so it does not actually distinguish the call. A reason does its job only when it points to a standard (the pasted standards, what you can observe, the audience’s needs) that another reader could apply the same way.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The reason points to no standard, so it would justify the opposite decision just as well\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"OVERRIDE is never a valid decision; the AI should always be accepted\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The row is missing a confidence rating\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing fails; a gut feeling is a sufficient reason in a reflection\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA reflection on teacher judgment is a concrete decision log, not a feelings paragraph. You record each moment where the AI advised something and you decided to accept or override it, with the reason. A freshman writes this as a four-column table so the judgment is countable. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. Write the weak reflection most students submit first:\n\n```text\nI learned a lot from using AI this week. It was really helpful and\nI feel more confident now. I will keep using it.\n```\n\nThat has zero decisions in it, so a reader cannot see any judgment.\n\nStep 2. Replace it with a decision log. One row per moment the AI advised you:\n\n| Situation | AI said | I decided | Why |\n|-----------|---------|-----------|-----|\n| Quiz feedback wording | \"Tell them they failed\" | OVERRIDE | too harsh for a first quiz; used \"scored 4/10, here is the plan\" |\n| Lesson order | \"Teach fractions before decimals\" | ACCEPT | matches the standards I pasted |\n| Parent email cause | \"Say the student is unmotivated\" | OVERRIDE | I cannot observe motivation; stated the missing assignments instead |\n\nStep 3. Count the pattern. Out of 3 AI suggestions, you overrode 2 and accepted 1. That ratio is itself evidence: it shows you were judging, not just following.\n\nStep 4. Write one sentence the log now supports: \"I overrode the AI twice when its advice assumed a cause I could not observe, and accepted it once when it matched the standards.\" The decision log plus that count is the evidence: a reader can audit every judgment call, not just a feeling.\n\nOrder the moves that turn a feelings paragraph into an auditable decision log:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Discard the feelings paragraph (’I feel more confident’) because it holds no decisions\", \"Make one row for every moment the AI actually advised something\", \"Record each decision explicitly as ACCEPT or OVERRIDE in its own column\", \"Give each decision a reason that points to a standard, not a mood\", \"Count the accept/override ratio and read it as evidence of weighing\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nNow turn one decision at a time into a defensible row, predicting each call before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build the decision log row by row\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The AI says ’tell the parent their child failed.’ You decide OVERRIDE. Which Why column makes the row auditable?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"’Too harsh for a first quiz; I used the observed score and a make-up plan instead’\",\"’I just didn’t like it’\",\"’Overriding shows independence’\",\"Leave the Why blank; the decision speaks for itself\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The reason that names a standard (proportional tone for a single quiz) and the concrete replacement is auditable. A reviewer can agree or disagree with the standard, which is what makes it judgment rather than mood.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The AI says ’teach fractions before decimals,’ and it matches the standards you pasted. What is the strongest call?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"ACCEPT, because it matches the pasted standards and you have no evidence against it\",\"OVERRIDE, to prove you are independent of the AI\",\"ACCEPT, but write Why: ’it felt right’\",\"Skip the row; accepts are not worth logging\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"ACCEPT with a standard-based reason. Accepting advice that matches a standard is also a defensible judgment; overriding good advice just to look independent is contrarianism, not judgment.\"},{\"reveal\":\"With the AI’s ’say the student is unmotivated’ overridden on the observability standard, your log shows 2 overrides and 1 accept, each with a standard-based reason. The ratio itself is evidence that you weighed the advice instead of rubber-stamping or reflexively rejecting it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nReflect by logging decisions, not feelings, and give every decision a reason tied to a standard. A reflection counts as evidence of judgment only when a reader can audit each accept and override independently.\n\nRun these three checks on your log:\n\n1. **Decision check:** Does each row name a real ACCEPT or OVERRIDE, rather than a general impression?\n2. **Reason check:** Does each reason point to a standard (observability, the pasted standards, audience needs) instead of \"it felt better\"?\n3. **Pattern check:** Does your accept/override ratio show weighing rather than blanket trust or blanket rejection?\n\nIf a row's reason would be just as true for the opposite decision, the reason is not doing its job and the judgment is not yet visible.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that more confidence equals better reflection. \"I feel confident\" is a feeling, and a confident wrong decision is worse than a hesitant right one. The log cares about the decision and its reason, not the mood around it.\n\nThe second misreading is that overriding the AI more often proves more independence. Overriding good advice for no reason is not judgment; it is contrarianism. The accept row in your log is just as important, because accepting advice that matches a standard is also a defensible call.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the decision log to a moment where the right call is to accept. The AI suggests breaking a long lesson into three ten-minute chunks with a check-in between each. Your instinct is to override, because you wanted to look independent. But the reason check stops you: the advice matches a real standard (short segments with frequent checks support attention), and you have no observed reason to reject it. So you log ACCEPT, with the reason \"matches the attention guidance in the module and I have no evidence against it.\"\n\nThat row is valuable precisely because it is an accept. It shows you can recognize good advice and take it, which is half of judgment. A log full of overrides would have hidden this skill. The walkthrough makes the point that judgment is not resistance; it is matching each decision to the evidence and standard, whichever way that points.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **decision** on the left, **the standard the reason invokes** on the right. A strong reader does not write \"good reflection\"; they write \"the override is defensible because the reason invokes observability, a standard that would apply again.\" Naming the standard tests whether the reason is principled or just a preference.\n\nA strong reader of Reflection on Teacher Judgment can answer four questions:\n\n1. How many real decisions does this reflection contain, not how many feelings?\n2. For each override, what standard does the reason invoke?\n3. For each accept, what evidence justified taking the advice?\n4. Does the accept/override pattern show weighing, or blanket trust or rejection?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can audit every row independently and judge whether each reason holds, because each row names the situation, the advice, the decision, and a standard-based reason. Include accepts as well as overrides so the log shows weighing rather than reflexive rejection.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"I overrode the AI twice where its advice assumed a cause I could not observe (motivation, effort), and accepted it once where it matched the standards I pasted. I rejected the AI's offer to add a confident-sounding summary line because confidence is not a decision and would have hidden the actual ratio.\" That ties the pattern to a principle and defends one rejection.\n\n> **Recent evidence:** Disclosing generative-AI use is now an explicit expectation in many courses and professional settings (2024-2026). MLA and APA style guidance directs writers to acknowledge not just that AI was used but which uses were made, and disclosure-based AI policies have become the standard approach on university syllabi. Your decision log doubles as exactly that disclosure artifact: it shows not just THAT AI was used but which suggestions were accepted, modified, and rejected, which is precisely what current disclosure norms ask for.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the decision log from classroom advice to a job-application context, where AI now suggests resume edits. The same structure applies: each suggested edit is a row, and you ACCEPT or OVERRIDE with a reason. But the standard shifts from \"what I can observe about a student\" to \"what I can defend about myself in an interview.\" An AI suggestion to add \"led a team of ten\" gets an OVERRIDE if you cannot back it up, for the same kind of reason an unobservable-cause claim got overridden in the classroom. The log structure and the discipline of standard-based reasons transfer; the standard you measure against changes with the audience.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how decision-log reflection connects to the chapter’s wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"learner_reflection\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the decision log packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-06-reflection-on-teacher-judgment-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"decision log\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the decision log.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the decision log for CAT 100 04.06. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_reflection_on_teacher_judgment\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your log has one row per decision, an explicit ACCEPT or OVERRIDE, a standard-based reason, and a count that shows weighing rather than blanket trust or rejection.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","concept_ids":["learner_reflection","cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"],"stem":"Which reflection actually shows teacher judgment a reader can inspect?","options":["I learned a lot and feel more confident using AI this week.","A decision log: 3 AI suggestions, 2 overridden and 1 accepted, each with a reason.","AI is really helpful and I will keep using it.","This week went well overall."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A decision log records choices and reasons; a feelings paragraph shows no judgment.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_feelings_not_decisions","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_reflection","concept_ids":["learner_reflection"],"stem":"A student's decision log shows 4 AI suggestions and 4 accepts, with no overrides. What does this reveal?","options":["The student judged each suggestion carefully.","The log shows following, not judging; at least one override with a reason should appear.","Nothing; accepting everything is ideal.","The AI must have been perfect."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"All-accepts with no override means no independent judgment is visible in the log.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_only_accepts","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","learner_reflection"],"stem":"Your log row reads: AI said 'tell the parent the student is unmotivated' and you decided OVERRIDE. Which 'why' makes the override defensible?","options":["I just did not like the AI's wording.","I cannot observe motivation, so I reported the missing assignments instead.","The AI is usually wrong.","I left the why blank because the override is obvious."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The reason ties the override to something you could not observe, which a reader can check.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_override_without_reason","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision","learner_reflection"],"stem":"Why does the override-to-accept count (for example 2 of 3 overridden) belong in a judgment reflection?","options":["It makes the reflection longer.","The ratio is itself evidence that you were judging the AI, not just following it.","Counts are required by the rubric only.","It hides the accepts from the reader."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A visible accept/override ratio lets a reader gauge how much independent judgment occurred.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_only_accepts","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reflection_on_teacher_judgment_reject_row","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"While you compile your decision log, the AI offers to add a confident-sounding summary line you did not ask for: 'Overall, my judgments consistently outperformed the AI's suggestions.' What is the strongest log entry?","options":["ACCEPT it; a confident summary polishes the log.","OVERRIDE it by toning the wording down slightly.","REJECT it, with the reason: an unrequested self-congratulatory summary would hide the actual accept/override ratio the log exists to show.","Leave it out silently; unrequested suggestions do not belong in the log."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The log's evidence is the pattern of decisions, including rejections. A flattering summary you did not ask for is itself a suggestion that must be judged, and it fails: it asserts a conclusion the rows may not support. Logging the rejection, with its reason, is what makes the judgment visible; silent omission erases the decision.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reject_not_logged","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-04-06-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_feelings_not_decisions","pattern":"reflection_as_feelings","description":"Writing a reflection that reports feelings ('it was helpful, I feel confident') with no recorded decision","trigger":"reflection names no situation where a choice was made","feedback":"A reflection on judgment logs decisions, not moods. Record each moment you accepted or overrode the AI and why.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_only_accepts","pattern":"no_overrides_recorded","description":"Logging only the times you accepted AI advice, never a time you overrode it, so no judgment is visible","trigger":"decision log has accepts but zero overrides","feedback":"If you never overrode the AI, you were following, not judging. Show at least one override with its reason.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_override_without_reason","pattern":"decision_without_justification","description":"Recording accept or override but leaving the 'why' column empty","trigger":"a decision row has no reason tied to a constraint or observation","feedback":"Every accept or override needs a reason a reader can check, such as a standard you pasted or a fact you could not observe.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_6_reject_not_logged","pattern":"unrequested_addition_accepted","description":"Accepting (or silently omitting) an unrequested AI addition instead of logging it as REJECTED with a reason, which inflates the artifact and hides a judgment call.","trigger":"the AI volunteers an extra line or claim you did not ask for and it ships, or disappears, without a log row","feedback":"Unrequested additions are decisions too. A confident-sounding extra line that you did not ask for gets its own log row: REJECT, with the reason. Silent omission hides the judgment; silent acceptance inflates the artifact. The log exists to show both.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"eb4ce0d6688097d3fe861ae0c0bddc1f3f1311b4893589f894dede040754c3af","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}